  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(1-3t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\,j]\\cdot (-3i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (1-3t)\\cdot (-3)+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} & =0 \\\\ -3+9t+2 & =0 \\\\ 9t-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=\\frac{1}{9}.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{1}{9})=(1-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9})\\,i+2\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}}\\,j=\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$",
